# CASES + PEOPLE TEMPLATES — full coverage pass (this round). Priority order
# per the owner's own instruction: case_detail.html + _side_section.html first
# (every tab, card header, button, field label, and the Reports/Reminders/
# Timeline machinery this same round's own status/enum stage only partly
# touched), then case_create.html (cases/forms.py's own CaseCreateForm labels
# and help text), archive.html + _archive_rows.html (column headers, the
# filter card, the status tabs), inbox.html (the dashboard most people land on
# first), then people/templates/people/person_list.html + _person_rows.html +
# person_shift.html + person_shift_month.html (people/forms.py's own
# PersonSearchForm, people/shift_hours.py's own off_reason strings), and
# finally accounts/templates/accounts/settings.html (accounts/forms.py's own
# SelfPasswordForm / AdminPlatformForm / AdminUnitStampsForm).
#
# "Off" the day-status word (person_shift_month.html: a day that is not a
# working day) is deliberately its OWN English source string, "Day off", not
# the bare "Off" the Unit-conversion / Manager-view TOGGLES elsewhere use for
# their own off state (_side_section.html, archive.html) — same English word,
# different meaning, and this hand-maintained catalog has no msgctxt support
# (see below), so a second source string is what keeps the two from colliding
# into one Persian word.
#
# EVERY {% blocktrans %}-DERIVED ENTRY BELOW IS KEYED ON THE REAL RUNTIME
# LOOKUP STRING, NOT THE TEMPLATE SOURCE TEXT: Django's own BlockTranslateNode
# .render() (django/templatetags/i18n.py) converts every {{ var }} inside a
# live blocktrans block into a %(var)s placeholder, and doubles any literal
# '%' to '%%', BEFORE ever calling gettext() — a catalog entry keyed on the
# literal "...{{ hours }}..." template text can never match that real lookup
# key ("...%(hours)s...") and gettext silently falls through to English. Caught
# live on the Settings page's own "Shift length" line during this round's own
# Persian verification pass, which is what sent every OTHER blocktrans entry
# below back for the same fix — each one regenerated by walking the touched
# templates' own COMPILED node tree (BlockTranslateNode.render_token_list(),
# the exact function Django calls at request time) rather than re-reading the
# template source by eye a second time.
#
# NOT translated on purpose, and left for a future round — see this round's
# own verification report for the full list: the Jalali date PICKER's own
# generated calendar text (static/js/jalali_picker.js — a separate, hand-rolled
# JS widget with its own English month names/"Today"/"Done", not wired into
# Django's i18n at all, and not the shared |jalali template filter's own output,
# which only ever prints numerals); the Jalali month-name generator itself
# (people/shift_hours.py::month_name_en, core/templatetags/ft_extras.py's own
# _JMONTHS, and the picker's identical table — three separate hand-maintained
# copies of the same English transliteration, e.g. "Farvardin", used across far
# more pages than this round's own five; changing what they PRINT is a shared,
# cross-cutting calendar change, not a template-chrome one); the accounts login
# page (accounts/templates/accounts/login.html) — confirmed BY READING
# accounts/middleware.py::LanguageMiddleware, deliberately: a signed-out request
# has no Profile to read a saved language from and this app deliberately never
# falls back to a cookie/session/Accept-Header (see that class's own docstring),
# so the login page can only ever render in settings.LANGUAGE_CODE regardless of
# what any template on it says — wrapping its strings would be inert; and every
# people/ screen this round's own priority list did not name (person_seats.html,
# request_detail.html, gm_overtime_inbox.html, _request_recent.html and others),
# which keep only the Status-adjacent strings the earlier status/enum-vocabulary
# round already gave them.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

